  "id": "Compound::DB00227 - Gene::3156",
  "verbalization": "Lovastatin, Pravastatin, and Rosuvastatin are all Hmg-Coa reductase inhibitors, a class of compounds that inhibit the enzyme Hmgcr, which is critical in the biosynthesis of cholesterol. The visible evidence suggests that Lovastatin and its analogs cause various adverse effects, such as facial paresis, hepatitis, and immune-mediated myopathy, which are all linked to the inhibition of Hmgcr. The shared mechanism of action—binding to Hmgcr—underlines the central role of this enzyme in these pathologies. The lowest common ancestors of Lovastatin and its analogs point to Hmg-Coa Reductase Inhibitor and Carbonyl Compound as key molecular features, reinforcing the structural and functional link between these compounds and Hmgcr. The consistent evidence across the three paths highlights a direct relationship between Lovastatin and Hmgcr, mediated through the inhibition of the enzyme, which is a defining characteristic of their pharmacological profile.",
